1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,960
Meet Mel.

2
00:00:01,960 --> 00:00:03,331
In our last episode,

3
00:00:03,331 --> 00:00:04,921
she tried to buy a toaster that

4
00:00:04,921 --> 00:00:06,094
wasn't made in China,

5
00:00:06,094 --> 00:00:07,270
and failed.

6
00:00:07,270 --> 00:00:08,827
So now she wants to build one

7
00:00:08,827 --> 00:00:09,473
herself,

8
00:00:09,473 --> 00:00:10,740
in Ohio.

9
00:00:10,970 --> 00:00:13,900
Picture her factory on a Sunday afternoon.

10
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:15,460
Robot arms working.

11
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:17,500
Cameras checking every part.

12
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:20,110
Software deciding what to build next.

13
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:22,960
And a price that matches China's.

14
00:00:22,990 --> 00:00:25,320
That factory doesn't exist yet.

15
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:27,646
But almost every piece of it already

16
00:00:27,646 --> 00:00:28,400
does.

17
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:30,813
So could AI make American factories

18
00:00:30,813 --> 00:00:32,380
cheaper than China's?

19
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:33,412
And if it did,

20
00:00:33,412 --> 00:00:35,800
would America actually win?

21
00:00:36,250 --> 00:00:43,167
If you like finding the hidden money behind the headlines, hit subscribe and like. It really helps the channel.

22
00:00:54,417 --> 00:00:56,807
Mel's banker isn't convinced.

23
00:00:56,807 --> 00:00:57,760
For forty years,

24
00:00:57,760 --> 00:00:59,477
the rule was simple.

25
00:00:59,477 --> 00:01:01,727
Build where the work is cheapest.

26
00:01:01,767 --> 00:01:04,347
But a robot doesn't care about wages.

27
00:01:04,347 --> 00:01:06,310
If machines do more of the work,

28
00:01:06,310 --> 00:01:08,467
that old rule starts to wobble.

29
00:01:08,467 --> 00:01:10,128
And the stakes are bigger than one

30
00:01:10,128 --> 00:01:11,077
toaster.

31
00:01:11,077 --> 00:01:11,727
Prices,

32
00:01:11,727 --> 00:01:12,152
jobs,

33
00:01:12,152 --> 00:01:13,944
and which country makes the things

34
00:01:13,944 --> 00:01:15,888
everybody needs.

35
00:01:16,150 --> 00:01:20,000
And America has already seen a preview.

36
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:20,667
Today,

37
00:01:20,667 --> 00:01:23,176
American factories make about twice as

38
00:01:23,176 --> 00:01:24,640
much as they did in the late

39
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:25,456
seventies,

40
00:01:25,456 --> 00:01:27,520
with millions fewer workers.

41
00:01:27,570 --> 00:01:28,330
More stuff,

42
00:01:28,330 --> 00:01:30,240
far fewer people.

43
00:01:30,290 --> 00:01:32,352
Automation is a big part of that

44
00:01:32,352 --> 00:01:33,364
story.

45
00:01:37,617 --> 00:01:39,592
So Mel calls her old friend Wei,

46
00:01:39,592 --> 00:01:42,567
the line engineer from our last episode,

47
00:01:42,567 --> 00:01:44,817
Why the World Still Depends on Chinese

48
00:01:44,817 --> 00:01:46,137
Factories.

49
00:01:46,137 --> 00:01:47,069
Wei's answer,

50
00:01:47,069 --> 00:01:48,147
in short.

51
00:01:48,177 --> 00:01:51,130
China makes about a quarter of everything

52
00:01:51,130 --> 00:01:52,796
the world manufactures,

53
00:01:52,796 --> 00:01:54,252
with suppliers down the street,

54
00:01:54,252 --> 00:01:54,863
giant ports,

55
00:01:54,863 --> 00:01:55,897
steady power,

56
00:01:55,897 --> 00:01:58,606
and engineers who've done it all before.

57
00:01:58,850 --> 00:01:59,780
And labor?

58
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:00,690
By our rough math,

59
00:02:00,690 --> 00:02:03,106
an American factory worker earns about

60
00:02:03,106 --> 00:02:04,778
five times what Wei's line workers make

61
00:02:04,778 --> 00:02:05,750
per hour.

62
00:02:06,040 --> 00:02:07,997
That gap is why Mel's old toaster

63
00:02:07,997 --> 00:02:09,954
was made in China in the first

64
00:02:09,954 --> 00:02:10,798
place.

65
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:13,699
So Mel's whole plan rests on a

66
00:02:13,699 --> 00:02:14,580
bet.

67
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:16,815
If robots shrink the labor part of

68
00:02:16,815 --> 00:02:17,535
the bill,

69
00:02:17,535 --> 00:02:20,370
does China's oldest head start shrink too?

70
00:02:20,460 --> 00:02:20,908
First,

71
00:02:20,908 --> 00:02:22,558
she needs to know what AI can

72
00:02:22,558 --> 00:02:25,279
really do on a factory floor.

73
00:02:29,517 --> 00:02:30,043
Kip,

74
00:02:30,043 --> 00:02:31,399
her consultant,

75
00:02:31,399 --> 00:02:33,937
starts the tour with what's proven.

76
00:02:34,217 --> 00:02:37,377
Cameras running AI software inspect every

77
00:02:37,377 --> 00:02:39,784
toaster faster than a human eye,

78
00:02:39,784 --> 00:02:41,729
and guide robot arms to grab parts

79
00:02:41,729 --> 00:02:44,517
that aren't perfectly lined up.

80
00:02:44,957 --> 00:02:47,939
The International Federation of Robotics,

81
00:02:47,939 --> 00:02:49,921
the industry's statistics body,

82
00:02:49,921 --> 00:02:52,322
calls machine vision industry-grade.

83
00:02:52,584 --> 00:02:53,075
Next,

84
00:02:53,075 --> 00:02:55,104
predictive maintenance.

85
00:02:55,104 --> 00:02:57,073
Sensors listen to a motor,

86
00:02:57,073 --> 00:02:59,664
and software flags it before it breaks.

87
00:02:59,764 --> 00:03:01,931
Smart scheduling reshuffles the line when

88
00:03:01,931 --> 00:03:03,614
an order changes.

89
00:03:03,874 --> 00:03:06,259
Self-driving carts move parts across the

90
00:03:06,259 --> 00:03:07,124
floor.

91
00:03:07,354 --> 00:03:09,440
The federation says tools like these are

92
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:12,126
increasingly used in industry.

93
00:03:12,384 --> 00:03:14,628
Then Kip shows her a digital twin,

94
00:03:14,628 --> 00:03:17,121
a virtual copy of a factory where

95
00:03:17,121 --> 00:03:19,387
engineers test the line before they build

96
00:03:19,387 --> 00:03:20,214
it.

97
00:03:20,294 --> 00:03:22,687
The electronics giant Foxconn says it

98
00:03:22,687 --> 00:03:24,841
built one with NVIDIA software for a

99
00:03:24,841 --> 00:03:26,970
new plant in Guadalajara,

100
00:03:26,970 --> 00:03:27,903
Mexico,

101
00:03:27,903 --> 00:03:31,136
to train its robots in simulation first.

102
00:03:31,384 --> 00:03:33,924
Let me sort what Mel just saw.

103
00:03:33,954 --> 00:03:34,646
Proven,

104
00:03:34,646 --> 00:03:36,291
the robot arms that weld,

105
00:03:36,291 --> 00:03:37,221
paint and pick,

106
00:03:37,221 --> 00:03:38,954
plus machine vision.

107
00:03:38,954 --> 00:03:39,605
Growing,

108
00:03:39,605 --> 00:03:41,154
predictive maintenance,

109
00:03:41,154 --> 00:03:43,754
AI inspection and smart scheduling.

110
00:03:43,754 --> 00:03:44,355
Not yet,

111
00:03:44,355 --> 00:03:46,842
general-purpose robots that learn any job

112
00:03:46,842 --> 00:03:47,994
by watching.

113
00:03:47,994 --> 00:03:50,551
The federation says that kind of physical

114
00:03:50,551 --> 00:03:52,643
AI isn't industry-grade yet.

115
00:03:52,884 --> 00:03:55,597
Mel asks about the humanoid robots from

116
00:03:55,597 --> 00:03:57,134
viral videos.

117
00:03:57,134 --> 00:03:59,058
The federation says most are still

118
00:03:59,058 --> 00:04:01,175
prototypes or pilot projects,

119
00:04:01,175 --> 00:04:03,291
and that for factory work today,

120
00:04:03,291 --> 00:04:06,070
ordinary industrial robots are the better

121
00:04:06,070 --> 00:04:06,884
deal.

122
00:04:07,094 --> 00:04:09,171
The robot that matters isn't the one

123
00:04:09,171 --> 00:04:10,304
that dances.

124
00:04:10,304 --> 00:04:13,225
It's the arm bolted to the floor.

125
00:04:13,484 --> 00:04:15,533
And here's why that arm matters less

126
00:04:15,533 --> 00:04:17,044
than Mel hoped.

127
00:04:17,244 --> 00:04:19,091
In American factories,

128
00:04:19,091 --> 00:04:21,353
out of every dollar of sales,

129
00:04:21,353 --> 00:04:23,636
only about thirteen cents go to wages

130
00:04:23,636 --> 00:04:25,484
and salaries for everyone,

131
00:04:25,484 --> 00:04:27,744
from the line to the front office.

132
00:04:27,914 --> 00:04:30,216
Materials and parts take by far the

133
00:04:30,216 --> 00:04:31,584
biggest bite.

134
00:04:31,817 --> 00:04:34,436
Watch what happens when I pull labor

135
00:04:34,436 --> 00:04:35,317
out.

136
00:04:35,317 --> 00:04:37,662
Even if every paycheck vanished,

137
00:04:37,662 --> 00:04:38,627
the materials,

138
00:04:38,627 --> 00:04:39,593
the machines,

139
00:04:39,593 --> 00:04:41,563
the energy and the building would still

140
00:04:41,563 --> 00:04:42,537
be there.

141
00:04:42,537 --> 00:04:44,342
And the robots that replace those

142
00:04:44,342 --> 00:04:46,747
paychecks send a bill of their own.

143
00:04:47,017 --> 00:04:49,507
Automation shrinks a slice.

144
00:04:49,507 --> 00:04:51,762
It doesn't make the factory free.

145
00:04:56,017 --> 00:04:58,447
So Mel runs an experiment.

146
00:04:58,447 --> 00:05:00,353
Her toaster costs a hundred dollars to

147
00:05:00,353 --> 00:05:02,641
make in a regular Chinese factory and

148
00:05:02,641 --> 00:05:05,147
ship to an American warehouse.

149
00:05:05,287 --> 00:05:07,078
Our model is illustrative,

150
00:05:07,078 --> 00:05:09,197
not any real company's costs.

151
00:05:09,197 --> 00:05:11,721
What matters is how it moves.

152
00:05:11,984 --> 00:05:12,462
First,

153
00:05:12,462 --> 00:05:14,914
the conventional Chinese plant.

154
00:05:14,914 --> 00:05:15,797
Cheap labor,

155
00:05:15,797 --> 00:05:16,907
suppliers next door,

156
00:05:16,907 --> 00:05:18,243
an ocean to cross,

157
00:05:18,243 --> 00:05:20,564
and a tariff at the border.

158
00:05:20,564 --> 00:05:22,652
That's Mel's benchmark.

159
00:05:22,917 --> 00:05:23,400
Next,

160
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:24,138
Kip's dream,

161
00:05:24,138 --> 00:05:27,537
a highly automated plant in Ohio.

162
00:05:27,547 --> 00:05:29,600
Robots cut most of the labor hours,

163
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:32,067
but American pay is higher.

164
00:05:32,087 --> 00:05:32,756
Robots,

165
00:05:32,756 --> 00:05:35,065
software and cameras add a big bill

166
00:05:35,065 --> 00:05:36,357
of their own.

167
00:05:36,437 --> 00:05:38,375
No ocean and no tariff on the

168
00:05:38,375 --> 00:05:38,999
toaster,

169
00:05:38,999 --> 00:05:41,537
but many parts still come from Asia.

170
00:05:41,567 --> 00:05:42,557
The total?

171
00:05:42,557 --> 00:05:44,502
Just two dollars more.

172
00:05:44,750 --> 00:05:46,190
Almost a tie.

173
00:05:46,190 --> 00:05:47,800
That's the headline.

174
00:05:47,830 --> 00:05:49,120
In a model like this,

175
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:51,737
automation can bring an American factory

176
00:05:51,737 --> 00:05:54,020
within a few dollars of China.

177
00:05:54,110 --> 00:05:56,630
Now watch what moves the winner.

178
00:05:56,884 --> 00:05:58,806
Raise the tariff a lot,

179
00:05:58,806 --> 00:06:01,634
and the Chinese plant gets pricier.

180
00:06:01,654 --> 00:06:03,454
Ohio wins.

181
00:06:03,494 --> 00:06:06,066
Now let Ohio's robots sit idle half

182
00:06:06,066 --> 00:06:08,724
the time because orders are weak.

183
00:06:08,974 --> 00:06:11,209
The cost of the machines behind every

184
00:06:11,209 --> 00:06:12,544
toaster doubles.

185
00:06:12,544 --> 00:06:14,304
China wins again.

186
00:06:14,474 --> 00:06:15,242
Tariffs,

187
00:06:15,242 --> 00:06:16,538
busy robots,

188
00:06:16,538 --> 00:06:17,835
imported parts,

189
00:06:17,835 --> 00:06:19,304
robot prices.

190
00:06:19,304 --> 00:06:21,294
Those are the switches.

191
00:06:21,550 --> 00:06:24,690
Then Wei calls back with another option.

192
00:06:24,690 --> 00:06:25,931
The same robots,

193
00:06:25,931 --> 00:06:27,290
in China.

194
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:28,596
Lower wages,

195
00:06:28,596 --> 00:06:30,133
suppliers down the street,

196
00:06:30,133 --> 00:06:32,999
and Chinese-made robots that we assume

197
00:06:32,999 --> 00:06:34,100
cost less.

198
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:36,804
It comes in about five dollars under

199
00:06:36,804 --> 00:06:38,142
Mel's benchmark.

200
00:06:38,384 --> 00:06:39,787
In our base case,

201
00:06:39,787 --> 00:06:42,964
Wei's automated factory wins.

202
00:06:43,084 --> 00:06:45,964
Ohio only comes out ahead when tariffs

203
00:06:45,964 --> 00:06:48,724
are high and its robots stay busy.

204
00:06:49,094 --> 00:06:51,474
So automation doesn't automatically favor

205
00:06:51,474 --> 00:06:52,754
America.

206
00:06:52,864 --> 00:06:55,083
It favors whoever automates best,

207
00:06:55,083 --> 00:06:56,294
at scale.

208
00:06:56,524 --> 00:06:58,664
Which raises the real question.

209
00:06:58,664 --> 00:07:01,431
Is China actually automating?

210
00:07:05,684 --> 00:07:06,784
It is.

211
00:07:06,784 --> 00:07:07,423
Last year,

212
00:07:07,423 --> 00:07:09,628
factories in China installed more

213
00:07:09,628 --> 00:07:11,832
industrial robots than the rest of the

214
00:07:11,832 --> 00:07:13,314
world combined.

215
00:07:13,374 --> 00:07:15,009
Compared with America,

216
00:07:15,009 --> 00:07:16,846
that's roughly nine robots for every

217
00:07:16,846 --> 00:07:18,364
American one.

218
00:07:18,404 --> 00:07:20,079
In a single year.

219
00:07:20,317 --> 00:07:21,937
Let me chart it.

220
00:07:21,997 --> 00:07:24,957
China's tower dwarfs everyone else.

221
00:07:24,957 --> 00:07:25,951
America,

222
00:07:25,951 --> 00:07:27,757
now the second-biggest market,

223
00:07:27,757 --> 00:07:29,777
looks like a stub next to it.

224
00:07:29,847 --> 00:07:30,714
And worldwide,

225
00:07:30,714 --> 00:07:32,692
installations hit a record,

226
00:07:32,692 --> 00:07:34,915
according to the federation's World

227
00:07:34,915 --> 00:07:36,627
Robotics report.

228
00:07:36,884 --> 00:07:38,534
Second surprise.

229
00:07:38,534 --> 00:07:40,341
More and more of those robots are

230
00:07:40,341 --> 00:07:41,844
made in China.

231
00:07:41,954 --> 00:07:43,731
A few years ago,

232
00:07:43,731 --> 00:07:46,442
foreign brands sold most of China's new

233
00:07:46,442 --> 00:07:47,414
robots.

234
00:07:47,414 --> 00:07:50,199
Now Chinese brands sell more than half.

235
00:07:50,450 --> 00:07:53,230
Now the twist inside the twist.

236
00:07:53,230 --> 00:07:54,687
Per factory worker,

237
00:07:54,687 --> 00:07:57,610
China isn't the most automated country.

238
00:07:57,670 --> 00:08:00,146
America has more robots per worker,

239
00:08:00,146 --> 00:08:02,434
and South Korea has several times more

240
00:08:02,434 --> 00:08:03,450
than both.

241
00:08:03,750 --> 00:08:06,339
China simply has so many factory workers

242
00:08:06,339 --> 00:08:07,912
that its robots are spread thin,

243
00:08:07,912 --> 00:08:08,790
for now.

244
00:08:08,790 --> 00:08:09,894
That's room to grow.

245
00:08:10,150 --> 00:08:12,560
And it has reasons to grow.

246
00:08:12,770 --> 00:08:15,807
The federation points to a labor shortage

247
00:08:15,807 --> 00:08:18,059
from an aging population,

248
00:08:18,059 --> 00:08:20,336
and China's new five-year plan aims AI

249
00:08:20,336 --> 00:08:23,720
research at physical uses like robots.

250
00:08:23,770 --> 00:08:26,840
Robots don't cancel China's advantages.

251
00:08:26,870 --> 00:08:29,142
Plugged into its supplier network,

252
00:08:29,142 --> 00:08:31,120
they may multiply them.

253
00:08:35,384 --> 00:08:36,934
Back in Ohio,

254
00:08:36,934 --> 00:08:40,064
Mel hits the supply-chain problem.

255
00:08:40,254 --> 00:08:42,756
Her factory can be brand new and

256
00:08:42,756 --> 00:08:44,994
still depend on the world.

257
00:08:44,994 --> 00:08:46,057
Robot arms,

258
00:08:46,057 --> 00:08:47,392
precision gears,

259
00:08:47,392 --> 00:08:48,183
sensors,

260
00:08:48,183 --> 00:08:48,701
chips,

261
00:08:48,701 --> 00:08:51,699
and the rare-earth magnets inside powerful

262
00:08:51,699 --> 00:08:52,844
motors.

263
00:08:52,994 --> 00:08:55,165
Build the factory at home,

264
00:08:55,165 --> 00:08:57,088
and you may still buy its brain

265
00:08:57,088 --> 00:08:59,611
and its muscles from abroad.

266
00:08:59,850 --> 00:09:02,329
And those suppliers live in different

267
00:09:02,329 --> 00:09:03,320
countries.

268
00:09:03,320 --> 00:09:04,926
Japan builds more of the world's

269
00:09:04,926 --> 00:09:07,500
industrial robots than anyone else.

270
00:09:07,500 --> 00:09:09,875
Germany and Switzerland are home to big

271
00:09:09,875 --> 00:09:11,700
automation suppliers.

272
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:14,332
America leads in AI software and chip

273
00:09:14,332 --> 00:09:15,280
design.

274
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:17,330
Taiwan and South Korea make many of

275
00:09:17,330 --> 00:09:18,210
the chips.

276
00:09:18,210 --> 00:09:20,387
And China refines most of the rare

277
00:09:20,387 --> 00:09:22,293
earths behind those magnets.

278
00:09:22,550 --> 00:09:24,415
Let me draw where the money from

279
00:09:24,415 --> 00:09:26,020
Mel's toaster could go.

280
00:09:26,020 --> 00:09:27,590
Materials and parts.

281
00:09:27,590 --> 00:09:28,860
The robot maker.

282
00:09:28,860 --> 00:09:30,220
Chips and sensors.

283
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:31,520
AI software.

284
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:32,310
Energy.

285
00:09:32,310 --> 00:09:33,480
Logistics.

286
00:09:33,480 --> 00:09:34,682
And whatever is left,

287
00:09:34,682 --> 00:09:36,501
for the factory owner.

288
00:09:36,750 --> 00:09:38,415
The splits in our picture are

289
00:09:38,415 --> 00:09:39,581
illustrative,

290
00:09:39,581 --> 00:09:41,370
but the lesson isn't.

291
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:43,494
The country that hosts the factory may

292
00:09:43,494 --> 00:09:45,490
not capture most of the value.

293
00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:47,352
In an automated world,

294
00:09:47,352 --> 00:09:49,824
money flows to whoever owns the robots,

295
00:09:49,824 --> 00:09:51,785
the software and the chips.

296
00:09:56,050 --> 00:09:56,497
So,

297
00:09:56,497 --> 00:09:59,323
could AI make American factories cheaper

298
00:09:59,323 --> 00:10:00,630
than China's?

299
00:10:00,710 --> 00:10:02,014
For some products,

300
00:10:02,014 --> 00:10:02,880
yes.

301
00:10:02,880 --> 00:10:05,286
The likeliest are heavy or bulky goods

302
00:10:05,286 --> 00:10:06,795
that are costly to ship,

303
00:10:06,795 --> 00:10:08,763
products built from many repeated

304
00:10:08,763 --> 00:10:09,835
precision steps,

305
00:10:09,835 --> 00:10:11,803
and things a country can't risk running

306
00:10:11,803 --> 00:10:12,437
out of,

307
00:10:12,437 --> 00:10:13,072
like chips,

308
00:10:13,072 --> 00:10:15,640
batteries and medical supplies.

309
00:10:15,770 --> 00:10:17,785
A toaster is light and cheap to

310
00:10:17,785 --> 00:10:18,263
ship,

311
00:10:18,263 --> 00:10:20,278
so Mel's choice is harder than she

312
00:10:20,278 --> 00:10:21,382
hoped.

313
00:10:21,617 --> 00:10:24,083
And if Mel does build in Ohio,

314
00:10:24,083 --> 00:10:26,576
her factory won't hire the crowds the

315
00:10:26,576 --> 00:10:28,007
old plants did.

316
00:10:28,197 --> 00:10:30,784
Economists Daron Acemoglu and Pascual

317
00:10:30,784 --> 00:10:33,372
Restrepo found that in American local job

318
00:10:33,372 --> 00:10:33,949
markets,

319
00:10:33,949 --> 00:10:35,521
the more robots arrived,

320
00:10:35,521 --> 00:10:37,512
the fewer people were employed and the

321
00:10:37,512 --> 00:10:39,107
lower wages went.

322
00:10:39,277 --> 00:10:40,847
The new jobs are different.

323
00:10:40,847 --> 00:10:41,828
Technicians,

324
00:10:41,828 --> 00:10:42,810
programmers,

325
00:10:42,810 --> 00:10:44,416
maintenance crews.

326
00:10:44,650 --> 00:10:45,638
Meanwhile,

327
00:10:45,638 --> 00:10:47,892
Wei's factory keeps its suppliers,

328
00:10:47,892 --> 00:10:48,626
its ports,

329
00:10:48,626 --> 00:10:49,614
its scale,

330
00:10:49,614 --> 00:10:51,640
and now the biggest fleet of new

331
00:10:51,640 --> 00:10:52,881
robots on Earth,

332
00:10:52,881 --> 00:10:55,000
most of them homemade.

333
00:10:55,300 --> 00:10:57,790
What could stop an American comeback?

334
00:10:57,940 --> 00:11:00,822
Robots sitting idle when demand is weak,

335
00:11:00,822 --> 00:11:02,251
missing parts suppliers,

336
00:11:02,251 --> 00:11:03,680
expensive money,

337
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:06,217
and trade rules that keep changing.

338
00:11:06,484 --> 00:11:08,224
So here's our verdict.

339
00:11:08,224 --> 00:11:10,416
AI doesn't end China's manufacturing

340
00:11:10,416 --> 00:11:11,454
dominance.

341
00:11:11,454 --> 00:11:13,314
It changes the test.

342
00:11:13,344 --> 00:11:14,423
For decades,

343
00:11:14,423 --> 00:11:16,404
the question was who has the cheapest

344
00:11:16,404 --> 00:11:17,444
workers.

345
00:11:17,584 --> 00:11:19,608
Now it's who can combine intelligent

346
00:11:19,608 --> 00:11:20,398
machines,

347
00:11:20,398 --> 00:11:21,795
reliable energy,

348
00:11:21,795 --> 00:11:24,022
patient capital and a complete supply

349
00:11:24,022 --> 00:11:24,824
chain.

350
00:11:25,054 --> 00:11:27,834
America can win back competitiveness,

351
00:11:27,834 --> 00:11:29,014
product by product,

352
00:11:29,014 --> 00:11:31,104
without winning back the same jobs.

353
00:11:31,104 --> 00:11:33,144
And China can automate too.

354
00:11:33,314 --> 00:11:35,093
Mel's choice won't be decided by the

355
00:11:35,093 --> 00:11:36,464
cheapest labor.

356
00:11:36,464 --> 00:11:38,932
It'll be decided by the best system.

357
00:11:39,184 --> 00:11:39,804
Next time,

358
00:11:39,804 --> 00:11:42,584
we follow the money behind another hidden

359
00:11:42,584 --> 00:11:44,824
link of the world economy.

360
00:11:44,824 --> 00:11:46,655
This is The Hidden Economics.
